"Number conserving cellular automata: from decidability to dynamics (https://arxiv.org/html/nlin/0102035)\nciteturn9682view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/nlin/0102035\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 690\nL0: ##### Report GitHub Issue\nL1: \nL2: [Button: ×]\nL3: \nL4: Title: [Input: Enter title]\nL5: \nL6: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below:\nL7: \nL8: Description:\nL9: \nL10: [Button: Submit without GitHub] [Button: Submit in GitHub]\nL11: \nL12: cite0†Back to arXiv L13: \nL14: cite1†Why HTML?†info.arxiv.org cite2†Report Issue cite3†Back to Abstract cite4†Download PDF L15:   1. cite5†Abstract L16:   2. cite6†1 Introduction L17:   3. cite7†2 Definitions L18:   4. cite8†3 A necessary and sufficient condition L19:   5. cite9†4 Dynamics L20:     1. cite10†4.1 Definitions and background L21:     2. cite11†4.2 Classification of dynamical behavior L22:     3. cite12†4.3 Examples L23:   6. cite13†5 Conclusions and open problems L24:   7. cite14†References L25: \nL26: cite15†License: Assumed arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license†info.arxiv.org L27: \nL28: arXiv:nlin/0102035v1 [nlin.CG] 27 Feb 2001\nL29: # Number conserving cellular automata:\nL30: from decidability to dynamics\nL31: \nL32: Laboratoire d’Informatique Théorique et Appliquée (LITA), I.U.T. de Metz, Île du Saulcy, 57045 METZ Cedex 01, France, roka@iut.univ-metz.fr\nL33: \nL34: Bruno Durand    Enrico Formenti Note: Laboratoire d’Informatique de Marseille (LIM), 39 rue Joliot-Curie, 13453 Marseille Cedex 13, France, bdurand@cmi.univ-mrs.fr, eforment@cmi.univ-mrs.fr Affiliation: Zsuzsanna Róka\nL35: ###### Abstract\nL36: \nL37: We compare several definitions for number-conserving cellular automata that we prove to be equivalent. A necessary and sufficient condition for cellular automata to be number-conserving is proved. Using this condition, we give a linear-time algorithm to decide number-conservation.\nL38: \nL39: The dynamical behavior of number-conserving cellular automata is studied and a classification that focuses on chaoticity is given.\nL40: \nL41: Keywords: Cellular automata, decidability, discrete dynamical systems\nL42: ## 1 Introduction\nL43: Cellular automata are widely known as formal models for complex systems ruled by local interactions [cite16†28 , cite17†25 , cite18†13 , cite19†23 , cite20†26 ]. Despite of their apparent simplicity they display a richness of behaviors most of which are not yet fully understood. The classification of these behaviors has a main obstacle: most of the interesting properties about them are undecidable [cite21†16 ].\nL44: Moreover, some other properties are “dimension sensitive”; for example, surjectivity is decidable in polynomial time for one-dimensional CA and undecidable for dimension $\\geq 2$ [cite22†15 , cite23†10 , cite24†8 ].\nL45: We prove that number-conservation, a widely used property, can be decided in linear time (in the size of the CA) in any dimension. More precisely, it has linear time complexity if we assume that the access to the transition table costs constant time, independently of the dimension of the space. Otherwise the complexity is $\\mathcal{O}{(s\\log s)}$, where $s$ is the size of the transition table.\nL46: A preliminary work had been carried out for one-dimensional CA on circular configurations (periodic boundary conditions) by Boccara et al. in [cite25†4 , cite26†3 ]. The decision problem in higher dimensions and general boundary conditions that we solved in the present paper is rather surprising and could not be expected from earlier works.\nL47: CA that satisfy number-conservation property are called number-conserving (NCA); roughly speaking they conserve the number of bits during their evolution. This class of CA has been introduced by [cite27†24 ] and is receiving a growing interest in Physics as a source for models of particles systems ruled by conservations laws of mass or energy.\nL48: Great attention has received the application of NCA to practical situations such as highway traffic flows. In this context different definitions of “number-conservation” were given according to the type of boundary conditions assumed (periodic, finite, infinite) [cite27†24 , cite25†4 , cite26†3 ]. In Section cite7†2 we prove that all of them are equivalent. We also give another equivalent definition in terms of densities. Section cite8†3 is devoted to the proof of our main result: the linear time decision algorithm.\nL49: In the second part of the paper we study NCA dynamics with focus on chaotic behavior. Number-conservation imposes strong “simplifications” in the dynamics allowing (in most cases) clear-cut situations that are not observed in the general case.\nL50: We stress that the class of NCA is not only big enough to give reliable models for car traffic and similar models allowing the study of traffic jams, critical traffic masses, crossing-roads, car accidents etc. But also they are interesting from a pure computer science point of view. For instance Morita et al. have given a reversible two-dimensional NCA capable of universal computation [cite28†20 , cite29†21 ].\nL51: ## 2 Definitions\nL52: \nL53: We present in this section several definitions of number-conserving cellular automata that we prove to be equivalent. Proofs are given in a one-dimensional space but can be generalized to any dimension.\nL54: Cellular automata are formally defined as quadruples $(d,S,$ $N,f)$. The integer $d$ is the dimension of the space the cellular automaton will work on. $S={\\left\\{0,1,\\ldots,s\\right\\}}$ is called the set of states. The neighborhood $N=(x_{1},\\ldots x_{v})$ is a $v$-tuple of distinct vectors of ${\\mathbb{Z}}^{d}$. The $x_{i}$’s are the relative positions of the neighbor cells with respect to the cell, the new state of which is being computed.\nL55: The states of these neighbors are used to compute the new state of the center cell. The local function of the cellular automaton $f:S^{v}\\mapsto S$ gives the local transition rule.\nL56: A configuration is an application from ${\\mathbb{Z}}^{d}$ to $S$. The set of configurations is $S^{{\\mathbb{Z}}^{d}}$ on which the global function $A$ of the cellular automaton is defined via $f$:\nL57: \nL58:  | $$\\forall c\\in S^{{\\mathbb{Z}}^{d}},\\forall i\\in{\\mathbb{Z}}^{d},A{(c)}{(i)}=f{\\left(c{(i+x_{1})},\\ldots,c{(i+x_{v})}\\right)}.$$  |\nL59: We denote by $\\mathcal{C}_{P}$ the set of (spatial) periodic configurations, (i.e., configurations that are periodic in all dimensions of the space). We denote by $\\pi(c)$ the period of a periodic configuration (i.e., a vector.) The expression $0\\leq k\\leq\\pi(c)$, $k\\in{\\mathbb{Z}}^{d}$ means $\\forall 1\\leq i\\leq d,\\ 0\\leq k_{i}\\leq\\pi(c)_{i}$ where $d$ is the dimension of the space and $k_{i}$ is the $i$-th component of $k$.\nL60: ###### Definition 1\nL61: \nL62: Let $A$ be a $d$-dimensional cellular automaton. $A$ is said to be periodic-number-conserving (PNC) iff\nL63: \nL64:  | $$\\displaystyle\\forall c\\in\\mathcal{C}_{P},\\ \\sum_{0\\leq k\\leq\\pi(c)}c(k)=\\sum_{0\\leq k\\leq\\pi(c)}A(c)(k).$$  |\nL65: \nL66: We denote by $\\mathcal{C}_{F}$ the set of finite configurations, (i.e., those configurations that are almost everywhere equal to zero — non-zero in a finite number of cells).\nL67: ###### Definition 2\nL68: \nL69: Let $A$ be a $d$-dimensional cellular automaton. $A$ is said to be finite-number-conserving (FNC) iff\nL70: \nL71:  | $$\\displaystyle\\forall c\\in\\mathcal{C}_{F},\\ \\sum_{i\\in{\\mathbb{Z}}^{d}}c(i)=\\sum_{i\\in{\\mathbb{Z}}^{d}}A(c)(i).$$  |\nL72: \nL73: We denote by $\\mathcal{C}$ the set of all configurations.\nL74: ###### Definition 3\nL75: \nL76: Let $A$ be a $d$-dimensional cellular automaton. A window is a hypercube of ${\\mathbb{Z}}^{d}$ centered in $\\mathbf{0}$ thus determined by its size. Consider the sequence of windows $\\{F_{n}\\}$ of size $2n+1$ and denote by $\\mu_{n}(c)$ the sum of states in $F_{n}$ of a configuration $c\\in\\mathcal{C}$. $A$ is said to be number-conserving (NC) iff\nL77: \nL78:  | $$\\forall c\\in\\mathcal{C},\\displaystyle\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\frac{\\mu_{n}(c)}{\\mu_{n}(A(c))}=1.$$  |\nL79: Remark that all these definitions imply that $f(0,0,\\ldots,0)=0$, i.e., $0$ is a quiescent state. The configuration with all quiescent states is denoted $uz$.\nL80: \nL81: We are going to prove that all previous definitions are equivalent. The proof is given in dimension 1; the generalization to higher dimensions is straightforward.\nL82: ###### Proposition 1\nL83: \nL84: A cellular automaton $A$ is FNC if and only if it is PNC.\nL85: \nL86: Proof. We first prove that if $A$ is PNC then it is FNC. We show that if $A$ is not FNC then it is not PNC either.\nL87: \nL88: Let $A$ be not FNC, then\nL89: \nL90:  | $$\\displaystyle\\exists\\widetilde{c}\\in\\mathcal{C}_{F},\\exists\\alpha,\\beta\\in{\\mathbb{N}},\\ \\alpha=\\sum_{i\\in{\\mathbb{Z}}}\\widetilde{c}(i)\\neq\\sum_{i\\in{\\mathbb{Z}}}A(\\widetilde{c})(i)=\\beta.$$  |\nL91: Let $k$ be the length of the non-zero part of $\\widetilde{c}$ and $l$ the radius of the neighborhood of $A$. Then, the length of the non-zero part of $A(\\widetilde{c})$ is at most $k+2l$ (see Figure cite30†1 ).\nL92: Let $\\widehat{c}$ be the periodic configuration constructed from $\\widetilde{c}$ in the following way. We add $l$ zeros at both sides of the non-zero part of $\\widetilde{c}$ and we construct a periodic configuration with the segment obtained (such a configuration has a period $p=k+2l$, see Figure cite30†1 ).\nL93: \nL94: cite31†Image: Refer to caption L95: \nL96: Figure 1: Construction of the periodic configuration\nL97: \nL98: We have\nL99:  | $$\\displaystyle\\sum_{0\\leq i\\leq p}\\widehat{c}(i)=\\alpha\\neq\\beta=\\sum_{0\\leq i\\leq p}A(\\widehat{c})(i),$$  |\nL100: \nL101: hence $A$ is not PNC.\nL102: \nL103: We now prove that if $A$ is FNC then it is PNC. We show that if $A$ is not PNC then it is not FNC either.\nL104: \nL105: Let $l$ be the radius of the neighborhood. As $A$ is not PNC,\nL106: \nL107:  | $$\\displaystyle\\exists p,\\alpha,\\beta\\in{\\mathbb{N}},\\exists\\widehat{c}\\in\\mathcal{C}_{P},\\ \\sum_{0\\leq i\\leq p}\\widehat{c}(i)=\\alpha\\neq\\beta=\\sum_{0\\leq i\\leq p}A(\\widehat{c})(i),$$  |\nL108: where $p$ is the period of $\\widehat{c}$.\nL109: \nL110: We denote by\nL111: \nL112: \nL113: \nL114: $\\widehat{c}_{p}$ the $p$-length segment of $\\widehat{c}$ centered at the origin;\nL115: \nL116: \nL117: \nL118: $\\widehat{c}_{p\\textnormal{Suffix}_{l}}$ the $l$-length suffix of $\\widehat{c}_{p}$;\nL119: \nL120: \nL121: \nL122: $\\widehat{c}_{p\\textnormal{Prefix}_{l}}$ the $l$-length prefix of $\\widehat{c}_{p}$.\nL123: We construct a finite configuration $\\widetilde{c}\\in\\mathcal{C}_{F_{A}}$ as shown in Figure cite32†2 : we repeat $\\widehat{c}_{p}$ $x$ times and we complete the segment obtained in this way with $\\widehat{c}_{p\\textnormal{Prefix}_{l}}$ at the right-hand-side and with $\\widehat{c}_{p\\textnormal{Suffix}_{l}}$ at the left-hand-side of the segment.\nL124: \nL125: cite33†Image: Refer to caption L126: \nL127: Figure 2: Construction of the finite configuration\nL128: \nL129: Then,\nL130:  | $$\\displaystyle\\sum_{i\\in{\\mathbb{Z}}}\\widetilde{c}(i)=\\underbrace{x\\sum_{i=1}^{p}\\widehat{c}_{p}(i)}_{x\\alpha}+\\underbrace{\\sum_{i=1}^{l}\\widehat{c}_{p\\textnormal{Prefix}_{l}}(i)}_{\\leq ls}+\\underbrace{\\sum_{i=1}^{l}\\widehat{c}_{p\\textnormal{Suffix}_{l}}(i)}_{\\leq ls}$$  |\nL131: \nL132: and\nL133:  | $\\displaystyle\\sum_{i\\in{\\mathbb{Z}}}A(\\widetilde{c})(i)$  | $\\displaystyle=$  | $\\displaystyle\\underbrace{x\\sum_{i=1}^{p}A(\\widehat{c}_{p})(i)}_{x\\beta}+\\underbrace{\\sum_{i=1}^{l}A(\\widehat{c}_{p\\textnormal{Prefix}_{l}})(i)}_{\\leq 2ls}+\\underbrace{\\sum_{i=1}^{l}A(\\widehat{c}_{p\\textnormal{Suffix}_{l}})(i)}_{\\leq 2ls}.$  |\nL134: \nL135: We obtain the following inequalities:\nL136:  | $$x\\alpha\\leq\\sum_{i\\in{\\mathbb{Z}}}\\widetilde{c}(i)\\leq x\\alpha+2ls\\textnormal{ and }$$  |\nL137:  | $$x\\beta\\leq\\sum_{i\\in{\\mathbb{Z}}}A(\\widetilde{c})(i)\\leq x\\beta+4ls.$$  |\nL138: \nL139: Suppose that $\\alpha<\\beta$. If we choose an integer $x$ such that $x>\\frac{2ls}{\\beta-\\alpha}$, we are in a contradiction with the previous inequalities.\nL140: \nL141: Similarly, if $\\alpha>\\beta$ then the contradiction is obtained by choosing $x>\\frac{4ls}{\\alpha-\\beta}$.\nL142: \nL143: Hence, if $A$ is not PNC, it is not FNC either. $\\square$\nL144: ###### Proposition 2\nL145: \nL146: A cellular automaton $A$ is NC if and only if it is FNC.\nL147: \nL148: Proof. We first show that if $A$ is not FNC then it is not NC either. Let $A$ be a cellular automaton with a neighborhood of radius $l$. If $A$ is not FNC then there exist an integer $\\gamma$ and a configuration $c$ in $\\mathcal{C}_{F}$ such that\nL149: \nL150:  | $$\\sum_{i\\in{\\mathbb{Z}}}c(i)=\\sum_{i=-\\gamma}^{\\gamma}c(i)\\not=\\sum_{i=-\\gamma-l}^{\\gamma+l}A(c)(i)=\\sum_{i\\in{\\mathbb{Z}}}A(c)(i).$$  |\nL151: Let $\\{F_{n}\\}$ be a sequence of windows of size tending to the infinity, centered on the non-quiescent part of $c$. As the non-quiescent part of $c$ is finite, there exists $k$ such that, for all $n\\geq k$,\nL152: \nL153:  | $$\\mu_{n}(c)=\\sum_{i=-\\gamma}^{\\gamma}c(i)\\not=\\sum_{i=-\\gamma-l}^{\\gamma+l}A(c)(i)=\\mu_{n}(A(c)).$$  |\nL154: \nL155: Hence, $\\displaystyle\\lim_{n\\to=\\infty}\\frac{\\mu_{n}(c)}{\\mu_{n}(A(c))}\\not=1$, so $A$ is not NC.\nL156: Now assume that $A$ is not NC. Then there exists a configuration $c$ such that $\\displaystyle\\frac{\\mu_{n}(c)}{\\mu_{n}(A(c))}$ does not converge to 1. Now let us call frontier of the window the stripe of width the diameter of the neighborhood at the window border. Let us now count $\\xi_{n}(c)$ the number of non-zero cells of $c$ in this frontier.\nL157: First case: $\\forall a,\\exists n,\\ \\mu_{n}<an$. Thus there exist consecutive subsequences of unbounded size in which $\\mu_{n}(c)$ is constant. Let us consider such a subsequence of length greater than the neighborhood. The frontier of the last window is thus full of zeros. Hence we can “cut off” the internal part, surround it by 0’s and thus obtain a finite configuration. As $A$ is not NC, it is not FNC either.\nL158: Second case: $\\exists a,\\forall n,\\ \\mu_{n}>an$. The idea is to “cut off” the windows to form periodic configurations. We can bound the perturbation on $\\mu_{n}(A(x)$ by the number of non-zero cells in the frontier multiplied by the maximal state (a constant). We then remark that with the hypothesis above, $\\xi_{n}(c)=o(\\mu_{n}(c))$. Thus for $n$ sufficiently big, the unbalance of the ratio $\\frac{\\mu_{n}(c)}{\\mu_{n}(A(c))}$ is transmitted to the periodic configuration obtained.\nL159: Therefore $A$ is not PNC and so not FNC. $\\square$\nL160: ## 3 A necessary and sufficient condition\nL161: \nL162: We present below a necessary and sufficient condition for number-conservation. Our condition is rather complicated to construct. For the sake of simplicity, we first present it in dimension 2 with a square neighborhood $2\\times 2$. Then we give the condition for neighborhood $n\\times m$ (proof sketched).\nL163: ###### Proposition 3\nL164: \nL165: Let $A=(2,N,Q,f)$ be a 2D cellular automaton with $N=\\left(\\begin{array}[]{ll}(0,0)&(0,1)\\\\\nL166: (0,-1)&(1,-1)\\end{array}\\right)$ and thus $f:Q^{4}\\to Q$. $A$ is number-conserving if and only if\nL167:  | $\\displaystyle f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}a&b\\\\\nL168: c&d\\end{array}\\right)$  | $\\displaystyle=a$  | $\\displaystyle+\\left[f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}0&0\\\\\nL169: 0&c\\end{array}\\right)-f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}0&0\\\\\nL170: 0&a\\end{array}\\right)\\right]$  |\nL171:  |  |  | $\\displaystyle+\\left[f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}0&0\\\\\nL172: 0&b\\end{array}\\right)-f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}0&0\\\\\nL173: 0&d\\end{array}\\right)\\right]$  |\nL174:  |  |  | $\\displaystyle+\\left[f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}0&0\\\\\nL175: c&d\\end{array}\\right)-f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}0&0\\\\\nL176: a&b\\end{array}\\right)\\right]$  |\nL177:  |  |  | $\\displaystyle+\\left[f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}0&b\\\\\nL178: 0&d\\end{array}\\right)-f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}0&a\\\\\nL179: 0&c\\end{array}\\right)\\right].$  |\nL180: Proof. We first show that the condition is necessary. Assume that $A$ is NC, let us denote by $\\natural c$ the sum of the states in a finite configuration $c$ of $A$. As $A$ is in particular FNC (see Prop. cite34†1 )\nL181: \nL182:  | $$\\natural A\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cccccc}...&...&...&...&...&...\\\\\nL183: ...&0&0&0&0&...\\\\\nL184: ...&0&a&b&0&...\\\\\nL185: ...&0&c&d&0&...\\\\\nL186: ...&0&0&0&0&...\\\\\nL187: ...&...&...&...&...&...\\end{array}\\right)=a+b+c+d.$$  |\nL188: \nL189: Moreover,\nL190:  | $\\displaystyle a+b+c+d$  | $\\displaystyle=$  | $\\displaystyle f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}0&0\\\\\nL191: 0&a\\end{array}\\right)+f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}0&0\\\\\nL192: b&0\\end{array}\\right)+f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}0&c\\\\\nL193: 0&0\\end{array}\\right)+f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}d&0\\\\\nL194: 0&0\\end{array}\\right)$  |\nL195:  |  |  | $\\displaystyle+f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}0&0\\\\\nL196: a&b\\end{array}\\right)+f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}c&d\\\\\nL197: 0&0\\end{array}\\right)+f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}0&a\\\\\nL198: 0&c\\end{array}\\right)+f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}b&0\\\\\nL199: d&0\\end{array}\\right)$  |\nL200:  |  |  | $\\displaystyle+f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}a&b\\\\\nL201: c&d\\end{array}\\right)$  |\nL202: Replacing $a$ by 0 yields:\nL203:  | $\\displaystyle b+c+d$  | $\\displaystyle=$  | $\\displaystyle f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}0&0\\\\\nL204: b&0\\end{array}\\right)+f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}0&0\\\\\nL205: 0&b\\end{array}\\right)+f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}0&c\\\\\nL206: 0&0\\end{array}\\right)+f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}0&0\\\\\nL207: 0&c\\end{array}\\right)$  |\nL208:  |  |  | $\\displaystyle+f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}d&0\\\\\nL209: 0&0\\end{array}\\right)+f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}c&d\\\\\nL210: 0&0\\end{array}\\right)+f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}b&0\\\\\nL211: d&0\\end{array}\\right)+f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}0&b\\\\\nL212: c&d\\end{array}\\right)$  |\nL213: Subtracting (cite35†3 ) from (cite36†3 ) we obtain:\nL214:  | $\\displaystyle f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}a&b\\\\\nL215: c&d\\end{array}\\right)$  | $\\displaystyle=a$  | $\\displaystyle+f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}0&0\\\\\nL216: 0&b\\end{array}\\right)+f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}0&0\\\\\nL217: 0&c\\end{array}\\right)+f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}0&b\\\\\nL218: c&d\\end{array}\\right)$  |\nL219:  |  |  | $\\displaystyle-f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}0&0\\\\\nL220: 0&a\\end{array}\\right)-f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}0&0\\\\\nL221: a&b\\end{array}\\right)-f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}0&a\\\\\nL222: 0&c\\end{array}\\right)$  |\nL223: Setting $b=0$ in (cite35†3 ), we have also:\nL224: \nL225:  | $\\displaystyle c+d$  | $\\displaystyle=$  | $\\displaystyle f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}0&0\\\\\nL226: 0&c\\end{array}\\right)+f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}0&c\\\\\nL227: 0&0\\end{array}\\right)+f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}0&0\\\\\nL228: d&0\\end{array}\\right)+f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}d&0\\\\\nL229: 0&0\\end{array}\\right)$  |\nL230:  |  |  | $\\displaystyle+f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}0&0\\\\\nL231: c&d\\end{array}\\right)+f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}c&d\\\\\nL232: 0&0\\end{array}\\right)$  |\nL233: \nL234: Subtracting (cite37†3 ) from (cite35†3 ) we obtain:\nL235:  | $\\displaystyle f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}0&b\\\\\nL236: c&d\\end{array}\\right)$  | $\\displaystyle=b$  | $\\displaystyle+f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}0&0\\\\\nL237: d&0\\end{array}\\right)+f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}0&0\\\\\nL238: c&d\\end{array}\\right)-f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}0&0\\\\\nL239: 0&b\\end{array}\\right)$  |\nL240:  |  |  | $\\displaystyle-f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}0&0\\\\\nL241: b&0\\end{array}\\right)-f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}b&0\\\\\nL242: d&0\\end{array}\\right)$  |\nL243: \nL244: We replace this term in (cite38†3 ):\nL245:  | $\\displaystyle f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}a&b\\\\\nL246: c&d\\end{array}\\right)$  | $\\displaystyle=$  | $\\displaystyle a+b-f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}b&0\\\\\nL247: d&0\\end{array}\\right)+f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}0&0\\\\\nL248: c&d\\end{array}\\right)-f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}0&0\\\\\nL249: a&b\\end{array}\\right)+f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}0&0\\\\\nL250: 0&c\\end{array}\\right)$  |\nL251:  |  |  | $\\displaystyle-f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}0&0\\\\\nL252: 0&a\\end{array}\\right)+f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}0&0\\\\\nL253: d&0\\end{array}\\right)-f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}0&0\\\\\nL254: b&0\\end{array}\\right)-f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}0&a\\\\\nL255: 0&c\\end{array}\\right)$  |\nL256: If $A$ is FNC then\nL257: \nL258:  | $$\\natural A\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cccccc}...&...&...&...&...&\\\\\nL259: ...&0&0&0&...&\\\\\nL260: ...&0&b&0&...&\\\\\nL261: ...&0&d&0&...&\\\\\nL262: ...&0&0&0&...&\\\\\nL263: ...&...&...&...&...\\end{array}\\right)=b+d.$$  |\nL264: \nL265: After similar calculi as above with the substitution of $b$ by 0, we obtain\nL266:  | $\\displaystyle f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}b&0\\\\\nL267: d&0\\end{array}\\right)$  | $\\displaystyle=$  | $\\displaystyle b+f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}0&0\\\\\nL268: 0&d\\end{array}\\right)+f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}0&0\\\\\nL269: d&0\\end{array}\\right)-f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}0&0\\\\\nL270: 0&b\\end{array}\\right)-f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}0&0\\\\\nL271: b&0\\end{array}\\right)$  |\nL272:  |  |  | $\\displaystyle-f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}0&b\\\\\nL273: 0&d\\end{array}\\right)$  |\nL274: \nL275: By replacing this term in (cite39†3 ), we obtain exactly the formula of our Proposition.\nL276: Let us prove now that this condition implies PNC. Then by Prop. cite34†1 , the theorem is proved. Consider a periodic configuration. Now sum this condition on the period; the terms $[f(\\ldots)-f(\\ldots)]$ simplify and we get exactly the condition for PNC hence for NC. $\\square$We now give the condition for more general neighborhood in dimension 2. We assert that an analogous formula can be obtained for higher dimension.\nL277: ###### Theorem 1\nL278: \nL279: Let $A=(2,N,Q,f)$ be a 2D cellular automaton with\nL280: \nL281:  | $$N=\\left(\\begin{array}[]{llll}(0,0)&(0,1)&\\ldots&(0,n-1)\\\\\nL282: (1,0)&(1,1)&\\ldots&(1,n-1)\\\\\nL283: \\vdots&\\vdots&&\\vdots\\\\\nL284: (m-1,0)&(m-1,1)&\\ldots&(m-1,n-1)\\end{array}\\right)$$  |\nL285: \nL286: and thus $f:Q^{m\\times n}\\rightarrow Q$. $A$ is number-conserving if and only if\nL287: \nL288:  | $$f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cccc}x_{1,1}&x_{1,2}&\\ldots&x_{1,n}\\\\\nL289: x_{2,1}&x_{2,2}&\\ldots&x_{2,n}\\\\\nL290: \\vdots&\\vdots&&\\vdots\\\\\nL291: x_{m,1}&x_{m,2}&\\ldots&x_{m,n}\\end{array}\\right)=x_{1,1}$$  |\nL292:  | $$+\\sum^{m-1}_{i=0}\\sum^{n-2}_{j=0}f\\left(\\begin{array}[]{cc}\\smash{\\makebox[0.0pt][l]{$\\left.\\smash{\\underbrace{\\makebox[0.0pt][l]{$\\begin{array}[]{ccc}0&\\ldots&0\\\\\nL293: \\vdots&&\\vdots\\\\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn9682view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/0012365X80901211\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nApproximating dynamics of a number-conserving cellular automaton by a finite-dimensional dynamical system (https://arxiv.org/html/2601.00479v1)\nciteturn9682view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2601.00479v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 443\n"
